    "updated_at": "2017-07-13T08:50:55Z",
    "body": "> Yeah, but also is this recommend by external style guides? It's not a construct I'm familiar with.\r\n\r\nYes it is recommended by pretty much all major style guides.\r\n\r\nFor example, in the [_Chicago Manual of Style_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Chicago_Manual_of_Style) (which is probably the most widely used general style guide for English in world—in particular by newspaper and magazine copy editors), the exact wording on the relevant guidance is this:\r\n\r\n> When a word or term is not used functionally but is referred to as the word or term\r\nitself, it is either italicized or enclosed in quotation marks.\r\n\r\nAnd the _Chicago Manual_ seems to be the main source that gets referred to in secondary discussions about what style to follow. For example, a quick search turns up:\r\n\r\nhttp://blog.dictionary.com/citing-words/\r\n\r\n> The _Chicago Manual of Style_ also says that when a word or phrase is used as a word, i.e., not used functionally but referred to as the word or term itself, it is either italicized or enclosed in quotation marks. The main guide is that both the word and its definition need to be set in either a different type (italic, underlined) or set inside punctuation marks (single or double quotations) so that they can be distinguished from the rest of the text.\r\n\r\nAlso from an FAQ at the online site for the _Chicago Manual_:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.chicagomanualofstyle.org/qanda/data/faq/topics/NoneoftheAbove/faq0012.html\r\n\r\n> Words used as words must be set off somehow—otherwise the meaning of the sentence can become ambiguous or even unintentionally funny:\r\n>\r\n>> He wrote the essay using fat instead of lard.\r\n>>\r\n>> It was ironic that the misspelled word was right.\r\n>>\r\n>> He wrote the essay using _fat_ instead of _lard_.\r\n>>\r\n>> It was ironic that the misspelled word was “right.”\r\n> \r\n> Chicago favors italics, but quotation marks are also fine.\r\n\r\nBut as far as any further guidance anywhere on which “words used as words” cases to use italics for vs. which cases to use quotation marks for, I was not able to find anything definitive.\r\n\r\nSee https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/44176/how-do-i-refer-to-a-word for example, which is… inconclusive.\r\n\r\nGiven that, I can imagine why the _Chicago Manual of Style_ doesn’t take a hard position on italics vs quotation marks for words as words—because it’s less important *how* the reference is set apart and more important that it just be set apart typographically in *some* way.\r\n\r\nSo for now I added https://wiki.whatwg.org/wiki/Style#Words_and_phrases_used_as_words to say:\r\n\r\n> When a word or term is not used functionally but is referred to as the word or term itself, either enclose it in quotation marks or in italics (using `<i>…</i>`).\r\n\r\nIf we want, I can refine that further to say that we prefer italics:\r\n\r\n> When a word or term is not used functionally but is referred to as the word or term itself, either enclose it in quotation marks or in italics (using `<i>…</i>`). _Italics are preferred._\r\n\r\nBut… I’m not sure we actually want to prefer italics, and in the particular case of the language this PR is patching, to my own Sprachgefühl at least it seems better to use quotation marks.\r\n\r\nAnd as I’ve said above, I think maybe it doesn’t matter so much which is used, as long as we set of the text typographically one way or the other.\r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
